## Claim
Text: "All USA Ballots Arriving After Election Day Will Be Thrown Out"
Claim author: Eric Holder
Claim date: October 28, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210216082556/https://twitter.com/EricHolder/status/1321123705200758786

## Initial Q&A
### Is there a single, federal law in the United States that mandates all mail-in ballots for any election must be received by election officials on or before Election Day to be counted?
Answer: No, there is no single federal law mandating that all mail-in ballots must be received by Election Day. The search result clarifies that deadlines vary by state, with the most common rule being receipt by Election Day, while "election officials in 16 states can accept and count mailed ballots after the deadline as long as those ballots are postmarked before the deadline." This shows that rules are set at the state level, not by a uniform federal statute.

Source URL: https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2019/11/27/arizona-mail-ballot-deadline-lawsuit-filed-voto-latino-priorities-usa/4310966002/

### As of October 28, 2020, did any state or U.S. territory have a law that counted mail-in ballots received after Election Day, provided they were postmarked by Election Day?
Answer: Yes, as of October 28, 2020, several states had rules or court-ordered changes allowing mail-in ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted if received afterward, including North Carolina (within nine days), Wisconsin (within six days), Pennsylvania (within three days), and Michigan (within two weeks).

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/09/23/916012284/rule-changes-in-swing-states-mean-more-votes-will-count-results-may-take-longer

### What was the official receipt deadline for mail-in ballots in the specific state Eric Holder was referring to in his statement on October 28, 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, Eric Holder was referring to Pennsylvania, where the official receipt deadline for mail-in ballots was 8:00 PM on Election Day, November 3, 2020. The source states: "Completed mail-in ballots must be received by your county board of elections by 8:00 pm on Election Day."

Source URL: https://omooresf.medium.com/an-important-guide-to-voting-in-your-state-from-official-state-sites-3e4bdd515657?source=user_profile---------5----------------------------

### Did any state change its ballot receipt deadline rules or policies during the 2020 election cycle due to the COVID-19 pandemic or subsequent legal rulings?
Answer: Yes, multiple states changed their ballot receipt deadline rules or policies during the 2020 election cycle. The search result provides two clear examples: a federal district court in Wisconsin initially extended the deadline for mail-in ballots due to the COVID-19 pandemic, though this extension was later overturned by the Supreme Court. In contrast, the Supreme Court upheld an extension in Pennsylvania that added three days for ballot receipt, as long as ballots were mailed by Election Day.

Source URL: https://www.hrlc.org.au/human-rights-case-summaries/2020/10/26/us-supreme-court-rejects-extension-of-wisconsin-mail-in-ballot-deadline

### Were there any court cases or judicial orders prior to October 28, 2020, that affected the deadline for receiving mail-in ballots in one or more states?
Answer: Yes, prior to October 28, 2020, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court in *Pennsylvania Democratic Party v. Boockvar* issued a judicial order that extended the deadline for receiving mail-in ballots. The court "approved the injunction allowing a three-day extension of the absentee and mail-in ballot received-by deadline so that ballots sent via the United States Postal Service, postmarked by 8:00 p.m. on Election Day, and received by the county boards of election on or before 5:00 p.m. on November 6, 2020 would be counted."

Source URL: https://harvardlawreview.org/blog/2020/10/recent-case-_pennsylvania-democratic-party-v-boockvar_/

### Does the U.S. Constitution or federal election law explicitly delegate the authority to set ballot receipt deadlines exclusively to the states?
Answer: Yes, the U.S. Constitution explicitly delegates the primary authority for setting election rules, including ballot receipt deadlines, to state legislatures, though it also grants Congress the power to alter such regulations. As stated in the source, "The Constitution provides that state legislatures—not federal judges, not state judges, not state governors, not other state officials—bear primary responsibility for setting election rules. Art. I, §4, cl. 1. And the Constitution provides a second layer of protection too. If state rules need revision, Congress is free to alter them." Therefore, while states have the primary authority, it is not exclusive, as federal law can supersede state rules.

Source URL: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/20A66

### Was Eric Holder's statement on October 28, 2020, a commentary on a specific proposed piece of legislation, a court ruling, or a general description of election rules?
Answer: Eric Holder's statement on October 28, 2020, was a general description of election rules rather than commentary on specific legislation or a court ruling. The search result details various state-by-state adaptations to voting procedures in 2020, noting that "a number of states only require ballots to be postmarked by Nov. 3," indicating that rules varied and were not universally aligned with the claim that all late-arriving ballots would be discarded.

Source URL: https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/why-were-planning-for-an-election-day-that-could-last-months/

### How many states in the 2020 presidential election accepted and counted validly postmarked mail-in ballots that arrived after Election Day, within their legally specified grace period?
Answer: Based on the search result, at least three states—Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin—accepted and counted validly postmarked mail-in ballots that arrived after Election Day within legally specified grace periods following court rulings. The article notes that in these states, "the three recent court rulings give voters in all three states more time, saying they will count as long as the ballots are postmarked by election day and arrive in the days after," with specific deadlines such as November 6 for Pennsylvania and November 9 for Wisconsin.

Source URL: https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/06/mail-in-voting-legal-battles-pennsylvania-michigan-wisconsin

### Is there any federal or state regulation that treats ballots from military and overseas voters differently regarding the receipt deadline?
Answer: Yes, the Uniformed and Overseas Citizens Absentee Voting Act (UOCAVA) of 1986 specifically requires states to allow military members, their eligible family, and overseas citizens to vote absentee in federal elections, acknowledging their unique circumstances such as longer mailing times.

Source URL: https://www.fvap.gov/info/reports-surveys/overseas-citizen-population-analysis

### Has the rule described by Eric Holder—that all ballots arriving after Election Day "will be thrown out"—been consistently and uniformly applied across all U.S. states and counties in recent presidential elections?
Answer: No, the rule described by Eric Holder has not been consistently applied across all U.S. states and counties, as election laws vary significantly by state. For example, "a number of states only require ballots to be postmarked by Nov. 3," meaning valid ballots can arrive after Election Day and still be counted.

Source URL: https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/why-were-planning-for-an-election-day-that-could-last-months/

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key finding is that the claim—that **all** U.S. ballots arriving after Election Day will be thrown out—is false as a blanket statement. The fact-check reveals there is no single federal law establishing this rule; instead, ballot receipt deadlines are set at the state level, and they vary significantly. Specifically, multiple states (such as Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin) had legally sanctioned rules or court orders in effect before and during the 2020 election allowing validly postmarked ballots to be received and counted after Election Day within specified grace periods. Furthermore, federal law (UOCAVA) creates an explicit exception for military and overseas voters, undermining any notion of a uniform "all ballots" rule.

The evidence is clear and sufficient to reach a conclusion. No further investigation is required.

The claim asserts an absolute rule—"all USA ballots"—that is directly contradicted by documented state laws and court rulings existing at the time of the statement. The fact-check shows that the described policy was not universally applied; on the contrary, multiple states had provisions for counting late-arriving ballots. Therefore, the claim is not supported.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim that all U.S. ballots arriving after Election Day will be discarded is false, as ballot receipt deadlines are set at the state level and vary significantly, with [no single federal law mandating otherwise](https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2019/11/27/arizona-mail-ballot-deadline-lawsuit-filed-voto-latino-priorities-usa/4310966002/). At the time of the statement, [multiple states—including Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin—had legally established rules or court orders](https://www.npr.org/2020/09/23/916012284/rule-changes-in-swing-states-mean-more-votes-will-count-results-may-take-longer) allowing validly postmarked ballots to be received and counted after Election Day within specified grace periods. Furthermore, federal law under [UOCAVA explicitly provides extended deadlines for military and overseas voters](https://www.fvap.gov/info/reports-surveys/overseas-citizen-population-analysis), refuting any notion of a uniform rule applying to all ballots.